{"card":{"card_id":"ma-c-835e699a2ad88a38f567","statement":"Our choice of notation is justified by the fact that when $X\\in \\pfpsp_k$ and when $\\eta_X=\\consdual_X$ is the usual canonical sheaf of $X$, then $\\La_X^\\eta=\\La_X$ is the usual constant sheaf on $X$, under the equivalence $\\shv(X,\\La)\\cong \\ind\\der_{\\ctf}(X,\\La)$ (see <<ref:eq:shv as indcons for pfp>>). In this case the right hand side of <<ref:eqn:characterization of verdier.pairing.placid.spaces>> is just <<ref:eq-verd-pairing>>. In particular, if $k$ is an algebraically closed field, then $\\rg^\\eta(X,-)$ given by the $*$-pushforward along $\\pi_X: X\\to \\pt=\\spec k$, and therefore fits into the paradigm of <<ref:rem-dualizability in corr>>.","source_id":"arXiv:2504.07482","cluster":"representation_theory","statement_sha256":"52b8cfc4ff5a7847cbd58c88fde732c417c8a96e9bb48fd84137c9fd026a97b6","kind":"remark","label":"Remark 764","ref_key":"rem:Verdier duality via dualizability in corr","name":"","order":1074,"deps":[{"ref":"eq-verd-pairing","card_id":"ma-c-96ff5544205d215ba332","label":"Equation eq-verd-pairing","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"eq:shv as indcons for pfp","card_id":"ma-c-7689416053faf3f8c77f","label":"Equation shv as indcons for pfp","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"eqn:characterization of verdier.pairing.placid.spaces","card_id":"ma-c-af64620f85a92101c104","label":"Equation characterization of verdier.pairing.placid.spaces","resolved":true,"target":"card"},{"ref":"rem-dualizability in corr","card_id":"ma-c-e0bcbed6f03837f239b4","label":"Remark 558","resolved":true,"target":"card"}],"cite_keys":[],"provenance":{"source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.07482","harvest_mode":"arxiv_eprint","payload_sha256":"1b700e8c4a2fc6f0c0f522039f223820ba6737a9a0ac91aea37c58ba7a3389c2","line":20097,"origin":"latex_env","retrieved_at":"2026-09-28T11:10:54+08:00"},"wash":{"version":"cardwash.v3","stages":["segment","purify","classify","dedupe","emit"],"numbering":"document_o